 it seems like 10 15 maybe  even closer to 20 year  this trend started around what I would  call kind of logistical help right which  was looking at the specifics of yoga and  meditation and a very kind of  nuts-and-bolts approach to feeling  better or whatever the case may be  and now in the last five or so years  there's been a lot more focus on what's  really what's the goal of all this stuff  that we're doing right as opposed to how  do you do a yoga pose more about well  what what is good work-life balance what  are the trends and socio in in culture  in the United States that maybe are  leading to left less satisfaction with  one's life do you see the same trend the  move away from the specifics of the yoga  or pilates and more an idea of how how  are our day-to-day lives affecting us  longer-term I definitely do I really  like that you'd say that I don't I  haven't heard many people really kind of  call it out explicitly but you know I  think for a long time it was about tools  and techniques it was all about like the  next cool thing to do and now it's like  okay everyone's looking back just like  you said saying okay well we've been  doing all this stuff but why what's it  for and what's it getting us you know  which is a excellent place to be because  we can all just go out and do a bunch of  cool stuff but what is it getting us and  I think it really kind of kind of points  toward this bigger desire to just like  have a more peaceful enjoyable  experience on the planet you know and  however that looks for you if it's if  it's more about success and achievement  or more about slowing down or speeding  up it's different for everyone but  seeing that all of those tools and  techniques of the past decade or so you  know are are all kind of pointing us  toward some bigger goal that you know  whatever that is for us in your  experience as a psychologist what trend  are you seeing in terms of what seems to  have people enjoying their lives less  than maybe they 